									</description></item><item><title>Big Nuclear isn’t worried about the Democrats stopping data centers</title><link>https://www.theverge.com/science/1034694/nuclear-energy-constellation-earnings-data-centers-democrats</link><guid>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1034694</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:29:37 -0500</pubDate><description>
											

						
&lt;figure&gt;

&lt;img alt="" data-caption="Constellation&amp;#039;s Clinton Clean Energy Center’s single nuclear reactor power plant, July 25, 2025 in Clinton, Illinois. Meta signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation for the output from the plant. | Image: Getty Images" data-portal-copyright="Image: Getty Images" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/gettyimages-2227026671.jpg?quality=90&amp;#038;strip=all&amp;#038;crop=0,0,100,100" /&gt;
	&lt;figcaption&gt;
	Constellation's Clinton Clean Energy Center’s single nuclear reactor power plant, July 25, 2025 in Clinton, Illinois. Meta signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation for the output from the plant. | Image: Getty Images	&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Constellation Energy - America's largest operator of nuclear power plants - does not share Wall Street's concerns that the Democrats will slow down data center development. On the company's third-quarter earnings call last week, CEO Joe Dominguez was, for lack of a better word, unbothered as he addressed the blue wave.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;"I do think we've seen a bit of an overreaction in the market to the election results based upon fear that Democrats won't let data centers get built," Dominguez said.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Many of the Democrats who seized control of the House and Senate in this month's midterm elections ran on anti-data-center campaigns, which triggered a &lt;a href="https://www.wsj.com/finance/stocks/wall-street-gets-2-5-trillion-ai-reality-check-with-historic-rout-of-tech-stocks-93985d7c?eafs_enabled=false"&gt;histor …&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.theverge.com/science/1034694/nuclear-energy-constellation-earnings-data-centers-democrats"&gt;Read the full story at The Verge.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
						
									</description></item><item><title>US data centers now burn more electricity than a midsized country</title><link>https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1034668/data-centers-electricity-climate-pollution-study-harvard</link><guid>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1034668</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:27:58 -0500</pubDate><description>
											

						
&lt;figure&gt;

&lt;img alt="Photo collage of a data center with data visualizations." data-caption="" data-portal-copyright="Image: Cath Virginia / The Verge, Getty Images" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/04/STKS528_DATA_CENTERS_B.jpg?quality=90&amp;#038;strip=all&amp;#038;crop=0,0,100,100" /&gt;
	&lt;figcaption&gt;
		&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Fossil fuels are still the biggest source of electricity for hyperscale data centers in the US, a new &lt;a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-026-01985-7"&gt;paper&lt;/a&gt; published in the journal &lt;em&gt;Nature Sustainability&lt;/em&gt; shows. They likely burn through more electricity collectively than many countries do, resulting in a serious amount of greenhouse gas emissions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The study homes in on hyperscale facilities - the massive structures spanning millions of square feet that developers are racing to build in large part to support generative AI. Researchers say the findings show that there's a lot of work left to do to cut down pollution stemming from AI's reliance on fossil fuels.&lt;/p&gt;
